<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nicene Christianity</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nicene <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> is the Christian doctrine defined by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-council-of-nicaea">Council of Nicaea</a> in 325 and the Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 381, which holds that the Son is of the same substance as the Father. Made the official faith of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a> in 380, it was opposed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arianism">Arianism</a>, which many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanic-peoples">Germanic peoples</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a>, had adopted. Conflict between the two shaped the kingdoms that succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nicene-christianity.jpg" alt="Nicene Christianity" width="320" height="433" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicaea_icon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Nicene <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> takes its name from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicene-creed">Nicene Creed</a>, formulated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-council-of-nicaea">First Council of Nicaea</a> in 325 and amended at the First Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 381. The dispute that set it apart from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arianism">Arianism</a>, its main rival at the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a>, concerned Christology, the nature of the divinity of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>. Nicene Christianity regards Jesus as divine and co-eternal with God the Father, while Arianism treated him as the first among created beings and inferior to the Father.</p><p>Religious historians and scholars often define Nicene Christianity more narrowly, as the first form of the state church of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> endorsed by the emperors. By that definition the Nicene Church ceased to exist after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-chalcedon">Council of Chalcedon</a> in 451, which was called to settle disagreements over the human and divine natures of Christ and concluded that he had two distinct but inseparable natures.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>After Chalcedon the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> made Chalcedonian Christianity its official religion, and the churches that held that Christ had a single nature were excommunicated by the Empire and became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oriental-orthodox-churches">Oriental Orthodox Churches</a>. Arianism, the faith of the Gothic kingdoms, was eclipsed during the 7th century as those kingdoms converted to Nicene Christianity.</p><p>In its broader sense the term covers every denomination that adheres to the teaching of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicene-creed">Nicene Creed</a>, which includes the vast majority of Christian churches today. Those outside it include both Protestant and non-Protestant nontrinitarian groups: most of the Latter Day Saint movement, though not the Community of Christ, formerly the Reorganized Church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus Christ</a> of Latter Day Saints, as well as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehovahs-witnesses">Jehovah's Witnesses</a>, the Unitarian Church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> and Oneness Pentecostals.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nicene Christianity” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 380 Theodosius I made <b>Nicene Christianity</b> the official religion of the empire, and pagan cults were soon banned.</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>In 380, under Theodosius I, <b>Nicene Christianity</b> became the state religion, and non-Christians faced exclusion from public life or persecution.</blockquote><a href="/religion/roman-religion">Roman religion · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In Spain it lost its last, probably already declining, role as a church language when the Visigoths converted from Arianism to <b>Nicene Christianity</b> in 589, though it survived as a language of the home in the Iberian Peninsula as late as the eighth century.</blockquote><a href="/language/gothic">Gothic · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>In 380 they issued the Edict of Thessalonica, making <b>Nicene Christianity</b> the only legal form.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-19">19 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>A leading defender of <b>Nicene Christianity</b>, he spent over 17 years in five exiles ordered by four different Roman emperors.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-9">9 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Earlier that year he had declared <b>Nicene Christianity</b> the faith of the empire, and he soon removed the city's Arian bishop.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-24">24 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
